Date: Wed, 23 Jun 1993 13:56:33 -0500
From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Craig Presson)
Subject: ARTS: "Extropian X"

In <9306231612.AA05860@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, Schirado writes:
|> Mark Desilets writes: "Given that Extropia has a certain set of
|> principles...it *is* possible to identify [music, literature, etc]
|> which exemplifies one of these principles."
|> 
|> NOT TO SLAM YOU, but I agree with Tim May on this one. This smacks
|> particularly strongly of Objectivism's claim that there can be such
|> a thing as "bad art" and "good art".[...]

Methinks I see some premises that need untangling. Any time you have
an ideology, you can, and people do, use that ideology to measure all
of experience. What you get when you do this, say to a particular
artform, is a spectrum from "strongly reinforces the ideology" through
"neutral" and "not applicable" to "inimical to the ideology". The
short step from this to "good" and "bad" is done by the ideologue by
translating "reinforces" to "good" and everything else to "bad", in
the time-honored methodology of the fanatic.

An ideology may draw on philosophy, politics, religion, or aesthetics,
and it may show more or less consistency. It may just comprise what
you default to by a lifetime of passive choices, and therefore find
hard to envision as an "ideology". But it is the trivial ideology,
against which "good" maps to "I like it" and everything else to "I
don't like it".

|> All art is inherently subjective. It is a subjective interpretation
|> of the external world or internal feelings, done by a unique
|> individual. All viewers/readers/listeners will respond to it in a
|> completely different fashion -- no two people will respond to it the
|> same way.[...]

Of course everyone's responses are colored by their experience,
education, culture, etc. However, people of common culture respond in
recognizably similar ways to certain things. Besides, there is an
artifact in any art, and that has an external objective existence, to
which nothing inheres. The artist takes his messy subjective
perceptions, makes a Ding an Sich, puts it in your way, and you take a
Ding an Sich and wrap it in your messy subjectivity -- but you're not
happy until you have done this, and forgotten the artist and his
artifact. Shame on you. After all, it takes conscious effort (or
intense and dangerous drugs ;-) to drop your baggage long enough to
_just look_ or _just listen_.

Art with enough "guts" to merit discussion works magic on the psyche,
focuses the inner vision to symbols and patterns invoked by the
artist. For art to _be_ (that word again) purely subjective as you
say, it must also be quite shallow -- or maybe just so incoherent that
it's a Rohrshach test.

Rock music, for example, invokes the libido, provoking the listener to
confront his (and especially her :-) "wild side". Having done this, it
can either leave you there (fine for party, lame for arty), or bring
in other symbols or archetypes to lead you to make new connections. If
this is done too heavy-handedly, you get preachy stuff (like a lot of
Rush) that is easily resented and rejected. Folk music similarly
invokes the shared past to predispose the listener to accept the
message of the song as if it came from his ancestors. If you like the
message, you're apt to let it slip past your defenses. In fact this
has a long standing as a means of cultural transmission. Music, magic,
firelight, sacred weeds or wines -- and whatever you are told the
first time you are admitted to this experience stays with you forever.

I had some rather nasty e-mail arguments with
|> people who claimed that by examining my tastes in music, literature,
|> etc., they could determine whether or not I was an Objectivist.. the
|> proper reply to which, of course, is, "Screw you and the horse you
|> rode in on."

No, that's a flip reply, not a proper one. I translate what you were
challenged to do as "provide evidence that your belief in and
committment to Objectivism has sufficient depth to guide your
aesthetic choices". Now, you may refuse to submit yourself to such
examination, or you may dispute the validity of the [O*]bjectivist
(that's the "closure" of big-O) analysis, but why dodge the question
like this? I don't approve of their cultish attitude, either, and
hereby hangs the point that Tim made: you don't _want_ a single
comfortable philosophy that you can use to make immutable choices that
never need revisiting. I enjoy the freedom to try on viewpoints -- but
this requires understanding them.

|> My philosophical beliefs are based on logic [...]
   My musical and literary preferences are sometimes based
|> on these things as well, but the vast majority of the time, they are
|> based on the simple fact that I enjoyed listening to/reading the
|> thing in question. And I don't need to justify my personal likes or
|> dislikes to anyone, for any reason [...]

Which is good, because almost by definition you can't justify what you
haven't examined. I prefer to "enjoy first, analyze later" rather than
"enjoy first, postpone analysis indefinitely". Of course, some things
cry out for analysis in real-time, such as visual art that uses
obvious symbolism.
