From: LEVY%BESSIE@Venus.YCC.Yale.Edu
Subject: SF: Linguistics themes

My all-time favorite SF book with a linguistics theme is 
_The Embedding_, by Ian Watson.  It's about a bunch of 
linguists, psychologists, and other scoundrels who 
perform "forbidden" experiments on refugee children from 
the Third World, in order to test various hypotheses 
about language and thought. It has a believable premise, 
a great plot, and is arguably Extropian in its attitude 
toward cognitive enhancement.  (A large part of the plot 
involves a South American tribe whose culture is based 
heavily on knowledge revealed through hallucinogens; 
Watson manages to avoid romanticizing them, much to his 
credit).  It is one of the few books I have read twice, 
and I recommend it highly.

As for Delaney, he doesn't know his aspirates from his 
elbow and only adds to the confusion and misinformation 
that the average intelligent reader has about linguistic 
terminology.  


-- Simon! D. Levy
