From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: Chomsky, Liars, and Dangerous Memes

On Mon, 21 Jun 1993 LEVY%BESSIE@venus.ycc.yale.edu wrote:

> >     And then there's Chomsky. The cases are not parallel in a couple of 
> > respects, most importantly that, IMO (nothing remotely humble here) Chomsky
> > *is* bogus in his own field.

> Although I sympathize with this viewpoint, I will reiterate my comment from an earlier 
> post that anyone who thinks it is easy to dismiss Chomsky's ideas should read R. Botha's
> _Challenging Chomsky: The Generative Garden Game_.  This highly entertaining book is 
> filled with examples of scholars who had a seemingly airtight, "common sense" 
> counter-argument to something that Chomsky had said in linguistics and who were shot down 
> so hard by Chomsky that their careers suffered as a result.

I'm not sure from what you say here that you may not be missing something
in Botha's rather fey tone in this book.  (A radical change from the 
discouragingly thick and plodding style of the rest of his work critiquing
generative grammar over the last 25 years, all of which was ignored by
Chomsky and most of the Chomskyan camp.  Partly because that's the way
they operate, partly I suspect because Botha is South African, which
furnishes the politically correct with a built-in excuse for not paying
attention to him.  (There was an open debate in _Cognition_, a journal
with decided Chomskyite leanings, during the late 70's about whether
they should have an explicit editorial policy of not accepting articles
from scholars in South Africa)).  Some of the challenges that he discusses
are in fact off the mark, but many of them are right on, and when Botha
sums up the reasons for dismissing them he is speaking sarcastically
in Chomsky's voice, not in his own.

> As for Chomsky's tactics, "intellectual thuggery" is not too far off the mark.  Two 
> favorite approaches are (1) making endless newspaper and journal citations -- seemingly 
> off the top of his head -- in order (I suppose) to make his opponents look uninformed, 
> and (2) ignoring a valid criticism and then silently incorporating it into his own work, 
> without acknowledging the contribution of the critic.

Both of these are characteristic of Chomsky, and decidedly sleazy, but
not necessarily "thuggish".  What I had in mind that deserves that 
description is his habit of misrepresenting his opponents' positions
in order to discredit them, and phrasing his arguments in such a way as to
pre-emptively characterize anyone who might disagree as stupid.

> So, although (or rather *because*) I have strong objections to Chomsky's work, I would 
> appreciate hearing from Scott (or anyone else) who can offer a counter-argument to some 
> specific thing that Chomsky has said about language.

Coming soon.


