Date: Wed, 23 Jun 1993 17:13:43 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: B-: SF: Linguistics

>                                               Suzette ??? Elgin, a
> retired linguist, has one or more novels based in part on linguistic work 
> on "women's speech" (I haven't read them, can't say more).

That's Suzette Haden Elgin; the novel is _Native_Tongue_, with a sequel
I've forgotten the name of.

>             Apparently [M. A. R. Barker] was the designer of a fantasy RPG in
> the 70's called (or about?) Tekumel, and the novel is a contribution
> to that mythos.  (All you gamers, anybody ever heard of this?)

Oh, yes, *indeed*.  Barker's Tekumel was the first, and (in my informed opinion
as an experienced player and dungeonmaster) still the best, of the FRPG world-
building efforts.  The original D&D system, the only one that pre-dated it,
was an ad-hoc collection of kluges for fantasy gaming with miniatures, not an
attempt to supply background for an entire and consistent secondary world with
a history and languages of its own.

The rules of the original game, "Empire of the Petal Throne", were not very
good (they've since been massively revised).  Barker's heart was in the maps,
the languages and the history.  And those were gorgeously exotic, light-years
more interesting than the medieval-mystery-meat/pale-ripoff-of-Tolkien
atmosphere of D&D and its clones.

Barker's central society, the Empire of Tsolyanu, combined the hydraulic
despotism of Imperial China with the blood-drenched splendor of the Aztecs.  
Adventurers could be feather-cloaked warriors in the ritual wars against the
Mu'ugalvyani, or foray against the mannerless paleskin barbarians of Yan Kor.
They could be priests attached to the vast temple-estates of the Twenty Gods,
or mages calling on the occult forces of the Thousand Planes, or artificers
attempting to understand the few remnants of technology from the days before
the stars went out.

>From the steaming jungles of Shenyu to the cold wastes of Nlyss; from the
Imperial Palace at Avanthar to Fasiltum, the ancient City of the Chiming Skulls;
from the busy port of Haida Pakala to the undead-haunted dungeons beneath
the Great Necropolis --- a character's path might lead to glory, or to the
impalement stake. 

Barker has published dictionaries of Tsolyani, histories of the major
political units and religions, and even a gruesomely fascinating demonology
called "The Book of Ebon Bindings".  The whole construct is time-deep in
an elaborate and detailed invented history stretching back thousands of years.

Even if you don't do RPGs, Barker's stuff is worth reading just for the
quality of the worldbuilding.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

