This is one version of what I have referred to as "quantum solipsism".

>    Anyone care to test this theory at the next extropians party? I'll
> invite Dr. Kavorkian.

Wouldn't prove anything.  You'd see your "subject" die in "your" universe.  He'd
still be alive in all of the universes where: he didn't attend the party, the
poison didn't work, the experiment wasn't tried, etc.

>   If such a theory could ever be validated (in all universes), it would
> certainly frustrate those who want to commit suicide.

Quite the contrary!  If this theory could be validated, suicide would suddenly
make a great deal of sense.  If, for instance, you decided that you would rather
kill yourself than live in poverty, then only non-poor versions of you would
exist in all of the possible worlds.  If you kill yourself whenever anything
unlucky happens, then all existing versions of you would be lucky.  With a
suitably painless and effective suicide method, if would make sense to kill
yourself rather than spend a Friday night without a date... :^)

