Date: Wed, 23 Jun 93 11:56:17 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Self-Reproduction, Complexity, and the Food Chain

The discussion of self-reproduction, or self-replication, has picked up
again here, especially with the comments of Nick Szabo and Derek Zahn (see
their posts for details, as I won't quote them here).

Now I maintain, and have long maintained, that the problem of
self-reproduction is quite hard, that the difficulties von Neumann faced
(and did not solve) have not been resolved. This is not to say they
_cannot_ be solved, as clearly self-reproducing machines of varying
complexities are all around us, and unless we believe in some kind of
self-reproductive vitalism, we will eventually be able to understand the
processes involved and emulate them with artificial constructs. Also, we
may be able to bypass certain constraints on biological self-reproduction,
just as we can build bridges and flying machines in ways much different
from those found in nature.

The problems are many:

- feedstocks, ecologies....what are the raw building materials? If I take
two factories, one producing Macintosh boxes and the other producing
Macintosh keyboards, and then claim that a third factory which plugs the
keyboard into the Mac is a "self-reproducing factory," clearly I have
cheated. (I'll describe this level of self-reproduction below.)

- the "key ingredient" problem...what limits the rate of self-reproduction?
In grey goo-like nano-self-replication, essentially nothing limits the
rate, except transport of raw materials (rocks, grass, people) to the
self-replicating goo. In higher-level systems, the rate may be limited by
availability of some constituent that cannot be manufactured by the
self-replicators....perhaps a microprocessor, perhaps some complex widgets,
etc.

- genome complexity may be very high. Even e. coli has 4 MB in its genome,
which represents a "point" (or cloud of points, in "quasi-species" terms)
in a "design space" of roughly 2^16,000,000 dimensions. (Granted, many of
the bits in any genome are useless, and don't code for self-replication, so
this estimate is indeed very rough. The main point is that the complexity,
the "logical depth" if you will, is much larger than might be suggested by
the mere storage requirements, which are modest.)

Here's a rough taxonomy of increasingly more-specialized self-reproducers.
Note that all of these are self-reproducers in real, physical space, using
real, physical components or raw materials. (I mention this because work on
abstract, mathematical self-reproduction--as with von Neumann's cellular
automata and the work that followed--is interesting, but is not true self
-reproduction of the sort that interests space colonizers, nanotech
enthusiasts, entrepreneurs, actual life studiers, and so on. Von Neumann
grappled unsuccessfully with the problems of 3D, real space
self-reproduction until Stan Ulam convinced him to shift his focus to
abstract automata. To my knowledge, no one has made any progress since in
physical self-reproduction, though of course the work on real life,
molecular biology, etc., is work in this direction.)


Level 1: Self-Reproduction from Inorganic, Widely Available Raw Materials

Crystals form a simple example, growing from a solution of raw molecules.
However, crystals lack complexity (low information content) and may be said
to have very low algorithmic information content (very compressible).

Drexler's vision of "grey goo" is this level: self-reproducing machines
dissassembling what they find on the planet (or Moon, etc.) and building
more of themselves. (The issue of program control is an interesting one: do
they carry their own instructions, do they have "helpers" (as with the
RNA-DNA ecology), or do they have an external, non-self-reproducing
computers sending instructions to all machines? Things get _much_ easier if
instructions can come from the outside, as von Neumann noted 35 years ago,
but of course this will likely "slow down" the process, perhaps
exponentially.)

Von Neumann space probes must operate at this level: landing on an alien
world and beginning to make a complete (perfect) copy from very low-level
raw materials.

Exponential growth can occur, subject to the machines getting out of each
other's way (!), because the "food" is, in a sense, pre-built. That is, the
food is lying around and does not have to be built. (When the food is
exhausted, the growth halts, of course.)


Level 2: Self-Reproduction from Simple Feedstocks

This is the main vision presented (to the extent self-reproducing general
assemblers are discussed at all) in "Nanosystems." Raw feedstocks of
chemicals, both organic and inorganic, are provided. Alcohols, esters,
sugars, etc.

Provided the raw feedstocks are easily built (and fairly cheap), this still
has most of the advantages of Level 1 self-reproduction (and some
advantages over Level 1, in terms of grey goo control). For example,
Drexler estimates (as Merkle would say at this point: "Wave
hands...grumble...wave hands...") that any gadget about the size of a
breadbox (a computer, another factory, etc.) could then be built for a few
dollars worth of sugar solution input and electricity.

(Of course, what makes up this breadbox factory is the interesting part!)

The algorithmic complexity (number of bits in the programs, in the building
of the constituent feedstocks, etc.) is, I maintain, completely unclear at
this time.

(That an organic virus can do it is a hint, but by itself is not yet a
complete answer. In line with my current interests, and with the "AIT
VirtSem" now starting up, I believe algorithmic information theory is a
natural starting point for analysis.)

A von Neumann probe could still be built at this level, provided it landed
on a planet with the right feedstocks (a gas giant, a comet, etc.). And if
the probe could "wait" (slower doubling time, which considering the travel
time, is reasonable), then low-efficiency processes for building the right
feedstocks could be used. For example, a von Neumann probe might land on an
Earth-like planet and then send seeds out to grow wild crops, or even to
farm nearby plots of land, to produce sugars, alcohols, etc., that it
needs. (Not exactly fast growth, but it still builds a double of itself, or
more than one, and hence the exponential expansion into the universe
continues. Exact rates are easy to calculate.)


Level 3: Self-Reproduction from Pre-Built Widgets

What I call the "hardware store scenario." Imagine a limitless supply of
screws, bolts, nuts, washers, rods, gears, metal sheets, cylinders,
whatever....all the stuff one would find in a large, well-equipped hardware
store. Even electric motors of various sizes and types (though part of me
would like to see what happens when the complexity of each widget is
constrained to fairly simple configurations, like the rods and gears
Drexler always cites).

Given this supply of widgets, of whatever size scale (I maintain that the
principles of general assemblers are scale-independent, though a room full
of clanking, friction-producing machinery may indeed be less reliable than
one built with  nanometer-scale components), design a self-reproducing
gizmo.

This gizmo will built a second gizmo (or will grow and split into two, as
with cell division, but I suspect this is unlikely for mechanical
components--they can't stretch, inflate, bend, etc., the way cells do).
Each of the resulting gizmos will then double, etc.

As expected, I think we have almost no knowledge of how complex such a
gizmo would be. A megabyte of descriptive code? A gigabyte? (I have heard
that the Boeing 747 is "describable" in some 250 megabytes of blueprints,
plans, specs, etc., but what this really means in terms of AIT is
unclear....some docs will make reference to "commonly understood" terms, or
other part numbers, screws, widgets, etc. An interesting problem in AIT:
describe the algorithmic information content of a Boeing 747.)

Such a self-reproducing machine at this level would still be interesting
and probably economically significant, especially if the "widgets" could be
built very cheaply. (An important point is that if the widgets
themselves--the gears, rods, motors, processors, etc.--are *not* being
built as part of the self-reproduction process, or are not being built by
_other_ self-reproducing plants (we admit the possibility of many types of
self-reproducing factories, obviously), then the supply of raw widgets
*becomes a rate-limiting process*.

That is, exponential growth is limited by the supply of raw materials. If
the input factories cannot produce widgets fast enough, the process slows
(and flattens out to a no-growth output rate).

In terms of von Neumann probes, the probe must now build factories which
make the widgets. As with the case above, exponential expansion throughout
the universe is still possible...it just takes a longer time (possibly much
longer, possibly not....).


Level 4: Self-Reproduction from High-Level Subassemblies

Here the problems  of raw material supply become even clearer. Conventional
factories must produce the subassemblies (microprocessors, robot arms,
lathes, etc.), and it becomes _almost_   secondary that a
"self-reproducing" factory can make a duplicate factory, and so on.

At some point it becomes pointless to call this self-reproduction.

However, our friend the von Neumann probe can still operate at this level,
provided it can--however slowly--build the factories it needs to build the
high-level subassemblies.


Level 5: Self-Reproduction from Very High-Level, Nearly Finished Subassemblies

The Macintosh factory that puts two halves together. The "self-reproducing
train" that links halves of trains together (a real example, from years
past). 

By this point, the idea of self-reproduction is completely lost, and it is
more accurate to simply say this is a normal factory. No exponential
increase is possible, as the non-self-reproducing parts of the food chain
dominate the production rate.

......

Well, that's a rough idea of the levels of self-reproduction. Living
organisms are almost always embedded in a complex soup or ecology of other
living organisms, with many levels of interaction, complicated food chains,
and the like.

(Still, many organisms can live and multiply in simple food environments,
such as sugars and agars.)

The complexity of ecologies and economies, and the costs involved at each
level, are, I think, profound questions.


-Tim May

